    <node id="&quot;HÀ NỘI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">""&lt;SEP&gt;""&lt;SEP&gt;""&lt;|"Capital city of Vietnam; mentioned as the location of Văn Miếu in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital area in northern Vietnam, located near the Đồng bằng sông Hồng."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; hosted the 2019 DPRK–US summit."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Đại Việt; mentioned as a northern strategic location near Đông Quan during the Ming occupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Vietnam where the VNDCCH was proclaimed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of Vietnam; mentioned in Vietnamese history context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital of the VNDCCH (North Vietnam)."&lt;SEP&gt;"City captured by French forces in 1873 during the campaign.""&lt;SEP&gt;"City where Đông Kinh Nghĩa Thục was established."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội (Hanoi), the northern capital, captured by the French in 1873 and 1882."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội is the capital of Vietnam and the political center from which Vietnam conducted its campaigns and diplomacy during the postwar era."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hà Nội, the modern capital of Vietnam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kinh đô cổ của Đại Việt, còn được gọi là Đông Kinh; bị chiếm đóng trong các chiến dịch liên quan đến Trịnh Tùng năm 1592."&lt;SEP&gt;"Modern name for the city that was once Đại La."&lt;SEP&gt;"Present-day capital of Vietnam; corresponds to ancient Tống Bình and later to Đại La."&lt;SEP&gt;"Địa điểm Pháp đồn trú và nơi VNDCCH sau này phát động tấn công các vị trí đồn trú của Pháp tại Hà Nội."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam, captured by French forces in the 19th century (1873 and 1882)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam, mentioned in the Vietnamese sources."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; central to the Lam Sơn uprising and Ming's occupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; referenced in the text in the context of border conflicts in the late 1970s."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of Vietnam; venue of the 2019 Korea–US summit."&lt;SEP&gt;"Capital city of modern Vietnam; historically the seat of the Đại Việt capital Thăng Long (also known as Đôn